    "detailedExplanation": "<h3>1. The Shungas & Indo-Greeks</h3>\n<p><strong>Pushyamitra Shunga (185\u2013149 BCE):</strong> Founded the Shunga dynasty after slaying Brihadratha; performed two Ashvamedha sacrifices (attested by Patanjali's <em>Mahabhasya</em> and the Ayodhya Inscription of Dhanadeva). Built the stone railings of the Bharhut Stupa.</p>\n<p><strong>Indo-Greeks (Bactrian Greeks):</strong> First to issue gold coins with portraits and legends in India. The most famous king was <strong>Menander (Milinda, 165\u2013145 BCE)</strong>, whose philosophical dialogues with Buddhist monk Nagasena form the celebrated Pali text <em>Milindapanho</em> (Questions of Milinda).</p>\n<h3>2. The Shakas & Western Kshatrapas</h3>\n<p>The Shakas (Scythians) established five branches in India. The greatest ruler was <strong>Rudradaman I (130\u2013150 CE)</strong> of the Kardamaka dynasty of Malwa. His <strong>Junagadh Rock Inscription</strong> in Gujarat is the first long epigraphic inscription in chaste Classical Sanskrit, recording his repair of the Sudarshana Lake (originally built under Chandragupta Maurya and completed under Ashoka).</p>\n<h3>3. The Kushana Empire & Kanishka (78 CE)</h3>\n<p>Hailing from the Yuezhi nomadic confederation, the Kushanas reached zenith under <strong>Kanishka I (reigned c. 78\u2013101 CE)</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Started the <strong>Shaka Era in 78 CE</strong>, adopted by the Government of India as the National Calendar in 1957.</li>\n  <li>Controlled the trans-Asian Silk Route, issuing abundant high-purity gold coins (<em>Dinaras</em>).</li>\n  <li>Mathura in Uttar Pradesh served as his eastern capital and a flourishing hub of indigenous art (e.g. the headless statue of Kanishka wearing boots and coat).</li>\n  <li>Patronized the 4th Buddhist Council in Kashmir and hosted eminent scholars: Ashvaghosha (<em>Buddhacharita</em>), Nagarjuna (Madhyamaka philosophy), Vasumitra, and Charaka (Father of Indian Medicine).</li>\n</ul>",
