    "title": "The Sangam Age in South India: Cheras, Cholas, Pandyas & Sangam Literature",
    "subtitle": "Tamil Assemblies at Madurai, Three Dynasties, Tolkappiyam, Silappadikaram & Trade with Rome",
    "learningObjective": "Understand the political kingdoms of the Far South during the Sangam Age, the nature of the poetic anthologies, and thriving Roman maritime commerce.",
    "introduction": "The Sangam Age (c. 3rd century BCE \u2013 3rd century CE) represents the classical literary and political dawn of South India. Named after the royal assemblies (<em>Sangams</em>) of Tamil poets convened at Madurai under the patronage of the Pandya kings, the period witnessed the emergence of the 'Muventhar' (Three Crowned Kings): the Cheras, Cholas, and Pandyas.",
    "detailedExplanation": "<h3>1. The Three Sangam Kingdoms (Muventhar)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>The Cheras (Kerala coast):</strong> Capital: Vanji / Karur; Emblem: Bow and Arrow. Most famous king was <strong>Senguttuvan (Red Chera)</strong>, who founded the famous Pattini cult (worship of Kannagi as the ideal chaste wife).</li>\n  <li><strong>The Cholas (Cauvery delta):</strong> Capital: Uraiyur (famous for cotton) and Puhar (Kaveripattinam, major sea port); Emblem: Tiger. Greatest monarch was <strong>Karikala</strong>, who built the Kallanai (Grand Anicut dam) across the Cauvery and defeated a confederacy at the Battle of Venni.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Pandyas (Southern Tamil Nadu):</strong> Capital: Madurai on Vaigai river; Sea port: Korkai (famous for pearls); Emblem: Fish (Carp). Renowned for patronizing the three Tamil Sangams. Famous ruler: Neduncheliyan (Battle of Talaiyalanganam).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2. Corpus of Sangam Literature</h3>\n<p>Sangam poetry is classified into two thematic genres: <strong>Aham</strong> (inner world of love and emotion) and <strong>Puram</strong> (outer world of war, heroism, and royal generosity):</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Tolkappiyam:</strong> Authored by Tolkappiyar; the earliest extant Tamil grammar and poetics treatise.</li>\n  <li><strong>Ettuthokai (Eight Anthologies):</strong> Including Purananuru and Agananuru.</li>\n  <li><strong>Pattuppattu (Ten Idylls):</strong> Including Maduraikkanji and Pattinappalai.</li>\n  <li><strong>Post-Sangam Epics:</strong> <em>Silappadikaram</em> (The Jewelled Anklet, by Ilango Adigal, detailing the tragedy of Kovalan and Kannagi) and <em>Manimekalai</em> (by Sittalai Sattanar, detailing the Buddhist spiritual journey of Kovalan's daughter).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>3. Flourishing Maritime Trade with the Roman Empire</h3>\n<p>Direct maritime trade through the monsoon winds linked Tamil ports to the Roman Empire. Excavations at <strong>Arikamedu (near Puducherry)</strong> revealed a Roman trading colony with Arretine ware, Roman amphorae (wine jars), and Roman gold and silver coins (bearing Augustus and Tiberius portraits). The Sangam port of <strong>Muziris (Kodungallur, Kerala)</strong> was an emporium for black pepper (termed <em>Yavanapriya</em> by Romans).</p>",
