    "title": "The Gupta Classical Empire: Samudragupta's Prayag Prashasti (UP), Chandragupta II & Golden Age",
    "subtitle": "Imperial Re-unification, Harishena's Inscription, Navratnas, Kalidasa, Aryabhata & Cultural Zenith",
    "learningObjective": "Analyze the foundation and expansion of the Gupta Empire, evaluate Samudragupta's military campaigns recorded on the Allahabad Pillar, and examine the cultural and scientific achievements of the Golden Age.",
    "introduction": "Founded by Sri Gupta and consolidated by Chandragupta I (who assumed the title <em>Maharajadhiraja</em> and started the Gupta Era in 319\u201320 CE), the Gupta Empire inaugurated what historians term the 'Classical' or 'Golden Age' of ancient India. It was characterized by political stability, territorial consolidation, Sanskrit literary brilliance, scientific advances in astronomy and mathematics, and sublime sculpture at Sarnath and Mathura.",
    "detailedExplanation": "<h3>1. Samudragupta (c. 335\u2013375 CE) & The Prayag Prashasti (Allahabad, UP)</h3>\n<p>Termed the 'Indian Napoleon' by historian V.A. Smith, Samudragupta pursued aggressive imperial expansion (<em>Digvijaya</em>). His campaigns are immortalized in the <strong>Prayag Prashasti (Allahabad Pillar Inscription)</strong>, composed in elegant Sanskrit <em>Champu</em> style by his court poet and minister <strong>Harishena</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Aryavarta Conquest (North India):</strong> Adopted policy of <em>Prasabhoddharana</em> (violent extermination and direct annexation of 9 rulers including Nagasena and Ganapatinaga).</li>\n  <li><strong>Dakshinapatha Conquest (South India):</strong> Defeated 12 rulers (including Vishnugopa of Kanchi) but reinstated them after receiving tribute (policy of <em>Grahana-Mokshanugraha</em>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Forest Kingdoms (Atavika Rajyas):</strong> Reduced into tributary vassalage.</li>\n  <li><strong>Frontier States & Foreign Powers:</strong> Received tribute and subjection (<em>Atmanivedana</em>) from Shakas, Kushanas, and Meghavarna of Sri Lanka (who was granted permission to build a monastery at Bodh Gaya).</li>\n  <li>Issued 8 types of gold coins (e.g. Lyrist type, showing him playing the Veena; Ashvamedha type).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2. Chandragupta II 'Vikramaditya' (c. 375\u2013415 CE)</h3>\n<p>Consolidated the empire through matrimonial alliances (marrying Naga princess Kuberanaga and giving daughter Prabhavatigupta to Vakataka king Rudrasena II). Conquered the Western Shakas, ending three centuries of Kshatrapa rule, assuming the title <strong>Shakari</strong> and <strong>Vikramaditya</strong>, and issuing silver coins (<em>Rupaka</em>).</p>\n<p>His court was adorned by the celebrated <strong>Navratnas (Nine Gems)</strong>, including Kalidasa (<em>Shakuntala, Meghaduta</em>), Amarasimha (<em>Amarakosha</em>), Varahamihira (<em>Panchasiddhantika, Brihatsamhita</em>), Dhanvantari (medicine), and Shanku.</p>\n<h3>3. Scientific, Mathematical & Artistic Flowering</h3>\n<p><strong>Aryabhata (476\u2013550 CE):</strong> Authored <em>Aryabhatiya</em> and <em>Surya Siddhanta</em>; calculated the value of Pi (3.1416), formulated the decimal system and zero, and established that the Earth is spherical and rotates on its own axis, explaining solar and lunar eclipses.</p>\n<p><strong>Metallurgical Marvel:</strong> The Mehrauli Iron Pillar (Delhi), commemorating King Chandra (identified with Chandragupta II), stands rust-free after over 1,600 years.</p>",
