    "title": "Jahangir, Shah Jahan (Taj Mahal), Aurangzeb & Decline",
    "subtitle": "Golden Age of Painting, Golden Age of Architecture, Deccan Campaigns & Factors of Empire Collapse",
    "learningObjective": "Trace the imperial trajectory through Jahangir's aesthetic patronages, Shah Jahan's architectural monuments, Aurangzeb's orthodox policies, and the systemic causes of Mughal decline.",
    "introduction": "The 17th century represented both the zenith and the beginning of the structural decline of the Mughal Empire. Under Jahangir, miniature painting achieved naturalistic perfection. Shah Jahan elevated marble architecture to world renown with the Taj Mahal in Agra. However, Aurangzeb's twenty-five-year Deccan campaigns, agrarian rebellions (Jats, Satnamis, Marathas), and the Jagirdari crisis exhausted imperial resources.",
    "detailedExplanation": "<h3>1. Jahangir (1605\u20131627): Justice & The Golden Age of Painting</h3>\n<p>Installed the famous <strong>Chain of Justice (Zanjir-i-Adl)</strong> made of gold with 60 bells at Agra Fort, allowing any aggrieved subject direct access to the emperor. His reign saw the influential 'Nur Jahan Junta'. Jahangir was a master connoisseur of painting; under court masters like <strong>Ustad Mansur</strong> (specialist in birds, flora, and fauna) and <strong>Abul Hasan</strong>, portraiture and natural studies achieved international fame. European envoys <strong>Captain William Hawkins (1608)</strong> and <strong>Sir Thomas Roe (1615)</strong> obtained commercial trading rights at Surat.</p>\n<h3>2. Shah Jahan (1628\u20131658): The Golden Age of Architecture</h3>\n<p>Replaced red sandstone with white Makrana marble inlaid with precious gems (<em>Pietra Dura</em> technique). Transformed Agra and Delhi with monumental masterpieces:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>The Taj Mahal (Agra, UP):</strong> Mausoleum of Mumtaz Mahal on the Yamuna; designed by Ustad Ahmad Lahori; completed in 1648; UNESCO World Heritage Site.</li>\n  <li><strong>Moti Masjid (Agra Fort):</strong> Elegant white marble pearl mosque.</li>\n  <li><strong>Shahjahanabad & Red Fort (Delhi):</strong> Built Diwan-i-Aam, Diwan-i-Khas, and the legendary <strong>Peacock Throne (Takht-i-Taus)</strong> studded with the Kohinoor.</li>\n  <li><strong>Jama Masjid (Delhi):</strong> Grandest congregational mosque in South Asia.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>3. Aurangzeb Alamgir (1658\u20131707) & Imperial Overstretch</h3>\n<p>Usurped power after the War of Succession, defeating Dara Shikoh at <strong>Samugarh (1658, near Agra)</strong>. Embodying conservative orthodoxy, he assumed the title <em>Zinda Pir</em> (Living Saint), banned music at court, prohibited Jharokha Darshan, appointed <em>Muhtasibs</em> (censors of public morals), and <strong>re-imposed Jizya in 1679</strong>.</p>\n<p>His 25-year personal military campaign in the Deccan (1681\u20131707) against the Marathas, Bijapur (annexed 1686), and Golconda (annexed 1687) proved fatal: termed by historians the <strong>'Deccan Ulcer'</strong> (like Napoleon's Spanish Ulcer), it emptied the imperial treasury, ruined communication lines, and destabilized northern administration.</p>\n<h3>4. Systemic Causes of Mughal Decline</h3>\n<p>The empire rapidly fragmented following Aurangzeb's death in 1707 due to:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Jagirdari / Be-Jagiri Crisis:</strong> Shortage of cultivable Paibaqi land to distribute as Jagirs to an inflated number of Mansabdars (historian Satish Chandra).</li>\n  <li><strong>Agrarian Distress & Peasant Revolts:</strong> Excessive revenue extraction provoked uprisings by Jats (Gokla, Churaman in Mathura/Agra), Satnamis, Sikhs, and Bundelas.</li>\n  <li><strong>Rise of Maratha Power:</strong> Guerrilla warfare under Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj and the Peshwas crippled Mughal armies.</li>\n  <li><strong>Foreign Invasions:</strong> Nadir Shah sacked Delhi in 1739 (carrying off the Peacock Throne and Kohinoor), followed by Ahmad Shah Abdali's repeated raids.</li>\n</ul>",
